# Telemetry and Tracing

## Overview

A live AI request emits `:telemetry` events for the Agent request, each tool
call, and each model call. Every event carries the same request, Session,
and Thread IDs, so you can join them into one trace. This notebook runs one
offline request on an Agent Server, prints the events it captured, and joins
them by ID. By the end, this works:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
Enum.map(events, fn {name, _measurements, _metadata} -> name end)
# => [
#      [:jido, :ai, :agent, :start],
#      [:req_llm, :request, :start],
#      [:req_llm, :request, :stop],
#      [:jido, :ai, :tool, :start],
#      [:jido, :ai, :tool, :stop],
#      [:req_llm, :request, :start],
#      [:req_llm, :request, :stop],
#      [:jido, :ai, :agent, :stop]
#    ]
```

Jido AI owns the Agent, reasoning, and tool events. ReqLLM owns model
request, token, and cost events. Neither records prompts, model output, or
tool arguments.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

## Offline Model

The fake provider queues OpenAI Responses API replies with `Req.Test`. The
Agent Server makes the model calls from its own process, so shared mode lets
that process use replies queued here:

```elixir
Req.Test.set_req_test_to_shared()

defmodule MyApp.OfflineModel do
  def options do
    %{
      api_key: "offline-test-key",
      max_retries: 0,
      max_tokens: 128,
      temperature: 0.0,
      req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, MyApp.LLM}]
    }
  end

  def respond_with(bodies) do
    Enum.each(bodies, fn body -> Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, &Req.Test.json(&1, body)) end)
  end

  def text(text) do
    response(%{
      "type" => "message",
      "id" => "msg_1",
      "role" => "assistant",
      "status" => "completed",
      "content" => [%{"type" => "output_text", "text" => text, "annotations" => []}]
    })
  end

  def tool_call(name, arguments) do
    response(%{
      "type" => "function_call",
      "id" => "fc_1",
      "call_id" => "call_1",
      "name" => name,
      "arguments" => Jason.encode!(arguments),
      "status" => "completed"
    })
  end

  defp response(item) do
    %{
      "id" => "resp_1",
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "output" => [item],
      "usage" => %{"input_tokens" => 20, "output_tokens" => 5, "total_tokens" => 25}
    }
  end
end
```

Each reply reports 20 input and 5 output tokens.

## The Agent

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LookupOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/actions/lookup_order",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "lookup_order",
    description: "Look up the shipping status of one order.",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string()})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{order_id: order_id}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{order_id: order_id, status: "shipped", eta: "2026-11-02"}}
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.SupportAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    name: "support_agent",
    description: "Answers order questions.",
    id: "my_app/support_agent",
    vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      model "openai:gpt-4o-mini"
    end

    tools do
      action MyApp.LookupOrder
    end
  end
end
```

## Attach a Handler

`Jido.AI.Telemetry.events/0` returns the full inventory, including the core
Jido, Runic, and ReqLLM events below it. This notebook attaches to the events
one request produces at the AI level:

| Event | Emitted |
| --- | --- |
| `[:jido, :ai, :agent, :start \| :stop \| :exception]` | Once per `jido.ai.request` Turn on an Agent Server. |
| `[:jido, :ai, :tool, :start \| :stop \| :exception]` | Once per tool call. |
| `[:jido, :ai, :reasoning, :start \| :stop \| :exception]` | Once per model round of a single-call or multi-stage Flow. |
| `[:req_llm, :request, :start \| :stop \| :exception]` | Once per provider call. |

ReAct model rounds don't emit reasoning events. The Agent and tool events
come from the Agent Server Turn, so `cmd/3` doesn't emit them.

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.TelemetryForwarder do
  def handle(event, measurements, metadata, pid) do
    send(pid, {:telemetry, event, measurements, metadata})
  end
end

ai_events =
  for kind <- [:agent, :tool, :reasoning], phase <- [:start, :stop, :exception] do
    [:jido, :ai, kind, phase]
  end

model_events = [[:req_llm, :request, :start], [:req_llm, :request, :stop]]

:telemetry.attach_many(
  "telemetry-guide",
  ai_events ++ model_events,
  &MyApp.TelemetryForwarder.handle/4,
  self()
)
```

The result is `:ok`. A handler that is a module function avoids the
`:telemetry` warning about local functions. Handlers run in the process that
emits the event, so keep them fast.

`Jido.AI.Telemetry.attach/3` attaches one handler to the complete inventory
when you want everything.

## Run One Request

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
{:ok, server} = Jido.start_agent(MyApp.SupportAgent, id: "support-live")

MyApp.OfflineModel.respond_with([
  MyApp.OfflineModel.tool_call("lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1001"}),
  MyApp.OfflineModel.text("Order A-1001 has shipped.")
])

signal =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Where is order A-1001?",
    options: MyApp.OfflineModel.options()
  })

{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(server, signal)

reply.data.status
```

The result is `:completed`.

## Read the Events

Collect what arrived:

```elixir
collect = fn collect, acc ->
  receive do
    {:telemetry, event, measurements, metadata} ->
      collect.(collect, [{event, measurements, metadata} | acc])
  after
    200 -> Enum.reverse(acc)
  end
end

events = collect.(collect, [])

Enum.map(events, fn {name, _measurements, _metadata} -> name end)
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[
  [:jido, :ai, :agent, :start],
  [:req_llm, :request, :start],
  [:req_llm, :request, :stop],
  [:jido, :ai, :tool, :start],
  [:jido, :ai, :tool, :stop],
  [:req_llm, :request, :start],
  [:req_llm, :request, :stop],
  [:jido, :ai, :agent, :stop]
]
```

Two model calls with one tool call between them, inside one Agent request.

```elixir
find = fn name ->
  Enum.find(events, fn {event, _measurements, _metadata} -> event == name end)
end

{_, agent_measurements, agent_metadata} = find.([:jido, :ai, :agent, :stop])

Map.take(agent_measurements, [:model_calls, :tool_rounds, :tool_calls, :input_tokens, :output_tokens])
```

The result is
`%{model_calls: 2, tool_rounds: 1, tool_calls: 1, input_tokens: 40, output_tokens: 10}`.

The Agent stop event carries the request summary as measurements:

| Measurement | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `duration` | Turn duration in native time units. |
| `model_calls`, `tool_rounds`, `tool_calls`, `tool_errors`, `retries` | Request counters from the reply. |
| `reasoning_steps` | Steps for Flows that report them, otherwise `nil`. |
| `input_tokens`, `output_tokens`, `cache_read_tokens`, `cache_write_tokens`, `reasoning_tokens`, `total_cost` | Request usage totals. |

```elixir
Map.take(agent_metadata, [:status, :reason_code, :agent_name, :agent_definition_id, :reasoning])
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  status: :completed,
  reason_code: :none,
  agent_name: "support_agent",
  agent_definition_id: "my_app/support_agent",
  reasoning: Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct
}
```

`status` is the reply status: `:completed`, `:blocked`, or `:failed`. A Turn
that crashed emits `[:jido, :ai, :agent, :exception]` with `status: :error`
and an `error_type`.

The tool stop event names the tool and the call:

```elixir
{_, tool_measurements, tool_metadata} = find.([:jido, :ai, :tool, :stop])

{Map.take(tool_metadata, [:tool_name, :tool_call_id, :action, :status]),
 is_integer(tool_measurements.duration)}
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{%{tool_name: "lookup_order", tool_call_id: "call_1", action: MyApp.LookupOrder, status: :ok},
 true}
```

## Correlation IDs

Every Jido AI event carries the same IDs:

| Metadata | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `request_id` | The request Signal ID, and `reply.data.request_id`. |
| `run_id` | One run of the reasoning Flow. |
| `session_id`, `thread_id` | The conversation. |
| `turn_id` | The Agent Server Turn. |
| `trace_id`, `span_id` | The Turn's trace context from core Jido. |
| `agent_id`, `agent_module`, `agent_name`, `agent_definition_id`, `agent_version` | The Agent. |

The Agent start event has the Agent, Turn, and request IDs. Tool events and
the stop event add `run_id`, `session_id`, and `thread_id`:

```elixir
{agent_metadata.request_id == reply.data.request_id,
 tool_metadata.run_id == reply.data.run_id,
 agent_metadata.thread_id == reply.data.thread_id}
```

The result is `{true, true, true}`.

ReqLLM events don't know about Agents. Jido AI passes the Thread ID to
ReqLLM as `telemetry: %{conversation_id: thread_id}`, and each model attempt
records ReqLLM's own request ID:

```elixir
model_stops =
  for {[:req_llm, :request, :stop], _measurements, metadata} <- events, do: metadata

{Enum.map(model_stops, & &1.request_options.conversation_id) |> Enum.uniq(),
 Enum.map(model_stops, & &1.request_id) ==
   Enum.map(reply.data.model_attempts, & &1.req_llm_request_id)}
```

The result is `{["session:support-live:thread"], true}`. Join Agent events
to model events on `thread_id` and `conversation_id`, and model attempts to
model events on `req_llm_request_id` and `request_id`.

The ReqLLM stop event carries usage and cost for one call:

```elixir
model_stops |> hd() |> Map.fetch!(:usage) |> Map.take([:input_cost, :output_cost, :total_cost])
```

The result is `%{input_cost: 3.0e-6, output_cost: 3.0e-6, total_cost: 6.0e-6}`.

Jido AI keeps a short-lived record of each active Turn to join these events.
Entries expire after `correlation_ttl_ms`, checked every
`correlation_sweep_interval_ms`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
config :jido_ai, :telemetry,
  correlation_ttl_ms: 3_600_000,
  correlation_sweep_interval_ms: 60_000
```

Those are the defaults. Each sweep that expires entries emits
`[:jido, :ai, :telemetry, :correlation, :cleanup]` with `expired` and `size`
measurements. A growing `size` means Turns aren't finishing.

## Metrics

`Jido.AI.Telemetry.metrics/0` returns `Telemetry.Metrics` definitions with
low-cardinality tags. Pass them to any reporter:

```elixir
Enum.map(Jido.AI.Telemetry.metrics(), &Enum.join(&1.name, "."))
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
["jido.ai.agent.stop.count", "jido.ai.agent.stop.duration",
 "jido.ai.reasoning.stop.count", "jido.ai.reasoning.stop.duration",
 "jido.ai.tool.stop.count", "jido.ai.tool.stop.duration",
 "jido.ai.model.request.count", "jido.ai.model.request.exception.count",
 "jido.ai.model.request.duration", "jido.ai.telemetry.correlation.expired",
 "jido.ai.telemetry.correlation.size", "jido.ai.model.input_tokens",
 "jido.ai.model.output_tokens", "jido.ai.model.cache_read_tokens",
 "jido.ai.model.cache_write_tokens", "jido.ai.model.reasoning_tokens",
 "jido.ai.model.total_cost"]
```

Agent metrics are tagged by `status` and `reasoning_name`, tool metrics by
`status` and `tool_name`, and model metrics by `provider` and `model`.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
children = [
  {Telemetry.Metrics.ConsoleReporter, metrics: Jido.AI.Telemetry.metrics()}
]
```

## Semantic Logs

Jido AI can log one line per event from the same telemetry, with IDs, status,
counts, and usage, and never content:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
config :jido_ai, :telemetry,
  semantic_log_mode: :interesting,
  semantic_slow_threshold_ms: 1_000
```

| Mode | Logs |
| --- | --- |
| `:off` (default) | Nothing. |
| `:errors` | Exceptions, `:failed` requests, and HTTP errors, as warnings. |
| `:interesting` | Errors, requests that didn't complete, and events slower than the threshold. |
| `:all` | Every Agent, reasoning, tool, and model request event. |

Errors log as warnings and everything else at debug level. A line looks like
`[jido.ai.semantic] event=ai.agent.stop agent_name=support_agent ... status=blocked`.

## OpenTelemetry

Add `opentelemetry_api` and an SDK, such as `opentelemetry` with an exporter,
to your application. When the SDK runs a real tracer at boot, Jido AI maps
each Agent request, tool call, and reasoning round to a span, and attaches
ReqLLM's model span bridge:

| Span | Parent | Key attributes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `invoke_agent support_agent` | none | `gen_ai.operation.name`, `gen_ai.agent.name`, `gen_ai.agent.id`, `gen_ai.agent.version`, `gen_ai.conversation.id`, `session.id` |
| `execute_tool lookup_order` | the Agent span | `gen_ai.tool.name`, `gen_ai.tool.call.id`, `gen_ai.tool.type` |
| `reasoning chain_of_thought` | the Agent span | `jido.ai.reasoning.method`, `jido.ai.model.request.id` |
| `chat gpt-4o-mini` | the Agent span | ReqLLM's GenAI client attributes |

Runtime IDs go under `jido.ai.*`, such as `jido.ai.request.id`,
`jido.ai.run.id`, and `jido.ai.turn.id`. A failed span gets `error.type`
and an error status. Spans opened inside a tool become children of the tool
span.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# mix.exs
{:opentelemetry_api, "~> 1.0"},
{:opentelemetry, "~> 1.5"},
{:opentelemetry_exporter, "~> 1.8"}

# config/runtime.exs
config :jido_ai, :opentelemetry, options: [req_llm: [content: :none]]
```

Model spans carry no prompt or output content by default. Set
`config :jido_ai, :opentelemetry, enabled: false` to turn the mapping off.
Jido AI doesn't start an SDK or configure sampling and export.

If your SDK starts after `:jido_ai`, attach the bridges yourself:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
if Jido.AI.Telemetry.open_telemetry?(), do: Jido.AI.Telemetry.attach_open_telemetry()
```

This notebook has no SDK, so the mapping is off:

```elixir
Jido.AI.Telemetry.open_telemetry?()
```

The result is `false`.

## Clean Up

```elixir
:telemetry.detach("telemetry-guide")
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("support-live")
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Summary

- Jido AI emits Agent, tool, and reasoning events under `[:jido, :ai]`.
  ReqLLM emits model events under `[:req_llm]`.
- Agent and tool events come from Agent Server Turns. The Agent stop event
  carries the request counters and usage.
- `request_id`, `run_id`, `session_id`, and `thread_id` join Jido AI events.
  `conversation_id` and `req_llm_request_id` join model events.
- `Jido.AI.Telemetry.metrics/0` feeds any `Telemetry.Metrics` reporter.
- Semantic logs and OpenTelemetry spans use the same events and carry no
  content.

